],\n      \"context_examples\": [\n        \"...his group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"ipa\": null,\n      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n      \"audio_url\": null,\n      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"glossary\": null,\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"structure\": {\n      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n      \"has_titles\": false,\n      \"has_parts\": false,\n      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n    },\n    \"plot_summary\": {\n      \"plot_summary\": \"In the face of a merciless plague known as the Red Death—characterized by bleeding from the pores and swift, agonizing death—Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and self-imposed isolation, retreats with a thousand courtiers into a secluded, fortified abbey. The abbey, a marvel of opulent design, features seven distinct chambers, each adorned in a different color and illuminated by firelight from tripods, with the final chamber shrouded in black and lit by blood-red stained glass. To escape the terror outside, the prince orchestrates a lavish masked ball, a defiant celebration of life and excess, where revelers dance in elaborate costumes, momentarily forgetting the horrors beyond the walls. The atmosphere is one of decadence and detachment, yet it is constantly interrupted by the ominous chimes of an ebony clock in the black chamber, which halts all merriment and casts a pall of dread over the festivities.\\n\\nAs the night deepens, the mood shifts from revelry to unease. The clock’s hourly tolls grow heavier, each chime a reminder of mortality’s inevitability. Then, a chilling figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, with a mask resembling a corpse and robes drenched in blood. This spectral intruder moves with eerie calm through all seven rooms, uninvited and unchallenged, a living embodiment of the plague they sought to flee. Prince Prospero, enraged by the violation of his sanctuary and the mockery of his defiance, pursues the figure with a dagger, driven by pride and fear. His pursuit leads him into the black chamber, where the clock’s final chime echoes as he collapses, dead—revealing the horrifying truth that the figure was not a guest, but the plague incarnate.\\n\\nThe revelers, now gripped by terror, rush into the black chamber only to find the mask and robes empty, confirming that the Red Death has infiltrated their sanctuary not as a visitor, but as an inescapable force. One by one, they fall, succumbing to the same fate as those outside the abbey. The clock stops, the firelight flickers out, and darkness consumes the abbey, marking the end of their illusion of safety. The story concludes with the complete annihilation of the court, underscoring the futility of attempting to escape death through wealth, power, or isolation. The final image is one of absolute stillness—a world where even the most elaborate defenses cannot withstand the inevitability of mortality.\",\n      \"themes\": [\n        \"identity\",\n        \"ambition\",\n        \"loss\"\n      ],\n      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n    },\n    \"model_usage\": {\n      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Character Extraction\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Character Profiles\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\